

Ronel Solomon 
Machine Learning Engineer 
Metaphor Data 
The project I worked on at Metaphor Data aims to address a common issue 
known as the cold start problem, which arises when new users must manually enter 
content into the system for it to be indexed and searchable. Currently, updates are 
unclear, and there is a pressing need for better organization, particularly within threads. 
My role involves developing a Slack/Teams bot design to retrieve all threads from a 
channel and identify conversations, deemed to contain institutional knowledge for the 
client. The primary objective is ensuring the accurate matching of assets with Slack 
messages. While some matching scenarios are successful, others may encounter 
issues. Some tasks I worked on in my internship included creating a post/notice to be 
executed manually from Slack, improving vector search criteria, and enhancing Slack 
URL retrieval for external clients. Ethics is important to this project because the Slack 
bot handles sensitive data that can be used to help get similar assets, make decisions 
without users' consent, and show fairness, transparency, and accountability. Throughout 
this paper, I will discuss many of the ethical implications of this project in Metaphor 
Data. 
When the Slack bot retrieves the message, Justice/Fairness and Privacy ethics 
say that the individual personal information should be respected and protected. My 
project at Metaphor data takes the user's data, to summarize and train the model to find 
an Asset. This may violate the Justice/Fairness perspective ethical lens. Moreover, this 
may raise privacy concerns as the bot should respect the users privacy concerns. Since 
Slack can save the whole message history, it is important getting the important data 
required to match the correct asset. Metaphor Data solves this issue while in the AWS 
logs, the company removes the owner of the conversation, so we do not see who wrote 
the message. They would change the name to be binary encoded. Something I noticed 
when doing my work. So when an Asset is saved in Metaphor Data, we will see the 
threads that relate to that Asset, but not know the names of those who wrote those 
messages. Likewise, if a name appears in the messages, there may be concerns. One 
of the options provided by Metaphor Data is that customers have different options to 
summarize the thread. When summarizing the thread. The model will remove the name 
and refer the name to a user. In that way, no personal names will be shown in the 
Metaphor app. This leads to care ethics by telling customers that Metaphor Data 
prioritizes the customer's privacy and security, before the bot. 
Another ethical concern, especially from the justice ethical lens, promote equal 
treatment and access to resources. Our goal is to prevent the risk of misuse of any 
personal data. The Slack bot has some security with unauthorized access. One way 
Metaphor Data and Slack handle this privacy concern is by allowing our customers to 
register the bot in a specific channel. If users allow the bot to enter the channel, they 
can read all the messages within that channel. Similarly, if a bot does not have access 
to a specific channel, it cannot read the messages within it.This allows fairness and 
opportunity for customers to engage with the bot from a specific channel. 
The next ethical concern in my project is the transparency and Accountability 
ethic of providing consent to both the Slack bot and the user. Based on the justice 
ethical lens, users should be informed on how their data is used all the time. Customers 

by the Virtue ethic should be aware of the full capabilities of the Slack bot, upholding 
their integrity. Following some of the principles of Explainable AI, Metaphor Data 
provides comprehensive documentation of the bot. Showing the user what the bot does, 
when certain conversations are flagged, and where the data goes. They even have a 
separate Slack channel and emails to allow customers to ask questions and concerns. 
Another ethical focus is obtaining consent to use people’s threads. From the app, Slack 
messages will be saved internally with the related asset. To address the issue of 
obtaining consent, customers are presented with a document to agree to during login 
like many web pages. Given the history of threads, some customers may have stopped 
using Metaphor. It would be considerate to notify them via email about the updated form 
indicating that their Slack messages might be used. This way, customers can be 
informed even if their messages are shared internally. Also, Metaphor Data should be 
professionally responsible and accountable with the Slack bot.  
Another ethical concern would be giving accountability to the Slack bot, 
especially assigning it the responsibility to find the correct asset for users. We wouldn’t 
want to anger customers by providing them with the wrong asset. Justice mentions 
“holding all persons and actions to the same moral standards” (Vallor, S., Raicu, I., & 
Green, B.). Additionally, our approach should follow the Utilitarian approach, where we 
weigh the happiness resulting from our actions. That is why I was tasked with creating 
another algorithm that detects if the message has a Related Asset. If the asset isn’t 
related to the message, I can display a message stating “No Asset is found”, instead of 
providing an incorrect asset match. My goal is to ensure customer satisfaction and 
provide the best possible asset. 
Furthermore, I aim to minimize the occurrence of "No Asset Found" messages. 
The ethical concern for fairness and bias arises when an asset is derived from a thread 
message that may not be related to the conversation. During my internship, I 
engineered a model to determine if the assets were related to a message. However, the 
model can be biased and misclassify the asset. Overall, I mitigated this bias was by 
creating a test set and evaluating precision/recall from it. Even with these performance 
results, there may still be perceived bias in the outcome. This enhances fairness within 
the bot. 
In the "Create a Post" feature, I allowed the user to suggest an asset by pressing 
a button. This grants users control over the bot's actions following the XAI guidelines. 
Therefore, giving transparency and care to users. In addition, we should present the 
description of the button in a format that is appealing to the customer. This way, the 
customer will feel comfortable with the Suggestion box. 
Another minor ethical concern to consider is the speed of asset retrieval. From 
the virtue ethic, customers should strive for excellence while working on this project. My 
task is to provide the fastest speed to obtain a related asset. We don’t want to waste 
resources on the computational cost of Metaphor Data or the customer's time. Priority is 
given to obtaining the asset in the fastest and most accurate manner possible. 
Throughout this paper, we discussed Justice/Fairness, Privacy, Care, 
utilitarianism, and Common Good ethics. Here, we see the Slack bot's goal is to benefit 
the user community by increasing the efficiency of retrieving resources faster, improving 
productivity by reducing time spent manually searching for information and making the 
bot faster, fair, accurate, and accessible to its users. Overall, we discussed not wanting 

to anger the user and how Metaphor Data addresses many of the ethical concerns, 
such as removing names. To continue my project, I don’t yet know the long-term 
impacts this new bot will have. There may be unidentified consequences that need to be 
addressed. I suggest that Metaphor Data continue to consider customer needs while 
striving to mitigate and improve the performance of my bot. As for my career, I learned 
to put myself in the customer's shoes when they use the product. 
 
 